+      **Two real application bugs found and fixed while writing the staff-permissions test, not
+      just a test written around existing behavior:**
+      1. The staff-permissions feature was previously cosmetic across the *entire* backend, not
+         just one route — every admin-mutating endpoint (products, green beans, order status and
+         refunds, settings, user management) accepted only `super_admin`, so a staff member
+         granted "Inventory" access would see the real panel and every button in it, then get a
+         real 403 on literally every action, every time. Fixed with a new `requirePermission(...)`
+         middleware, accepted alongside the existing `requireAdmin` — deliberately **not** applied
+         to `/users`, since that route also handles role/permission changes and there's no safe
+         subset of it to grant a staff member without creating a real privilege-escalation path
+         (a staff member with "Customers" access could otherwise promote themselves). Confirmed
+         directly, not assumed: a real test grants a staff account "Customers" specifically and
+         confirms `/users` still returns 403 either way, including a real self-promotion attempt.
+      2. Even after that fix, staff members had no way to *reach* the dashboard at all — the
+         nav's "Admin" button (desktop) and "Admin Dashboard" link (mobile) were both gated to
+         `super_admin` only, even though the page itself already correctly allowed staff through.
+         Fixed to include staff in both.
+      **Real, hard-won test-infrastructure debugging along the way, not glossed over:** the
+      staff-permissions test intermittently failed with no clear cause at first. Rather than
+      guess, each theory was checked against real evidence — Railway's live logs (ruled out rate
+      limiting: zero 429s anywhere), a manual sign-in in a plain browser tab (ruled out the
+      backend or account: worked instantly), and Playwright's own trace viewer's Network tab
+      (the actual proof: every real `/auth/login` request this suite ever made returned a clean
+      200, meaning the app was never at fault). The real, if less satisfying, explanation was
+      test-side flakiness in an unusually long chain of sign-in/sign-out cycles within one test —
+      hardened with a shared, retry-wrapped `signIn()`/`openAdminDashboard()` pair used
+      consistently everywhere the suite needs to reach the dashboard, not just the one spot that
+      happened to fail first. Separately, this file's frequent re-navigation gave the real,
+      external `ipapi.co` geo-lookup call (already known to need blocking — see the currency
+      auto-detection bullet above) far more chances to introduce genuine slowness; now blocked
+      here too, the same way `admin.spec.js` already does it.
